<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>A good memory<br />isn’t a library.</h2>
  <p>A model keeps learned patterns in its <strong>parameters</strong>: numbers changed during training. Updating a fact inside those numbers is harder than replacing a library notice.</p>
  <p>Could a model look something up before answering?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>Look first.<br />Then write.</h2>
  <p>Patrick Lewis and colleagues introduced <strong>retrieval-augmented generation</strong>, or RAG, in 2020. A <strong>retriever</strong> finds useful text. A <strong>generator</strong> uses it to write an answer.</p>
  <p>The library was Wikipedia. Questions and passages became <strong>vectors</strong>, lists of numbers used to score their match. BART, a model trained to read and write text, generated the answer. Training adjusted the question-searching and writing parts together.</p>
  <p>RAG kept knowledge in learned parameters and an external library. Replacing the library needed no retraining. Finding a passage still did not guarantee a correct answer.</p>
