</section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play">
  <p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p>
  <h2>Poison<br />the library.</h2><Discovery slug="retrieval" />
  <p>The reader trusts whatever the search hands it. Plant a page that makes it give the wrong opening time with full confidence, in as few edits as you can. Then switch sides and win the true answer back.</p>
  <noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to edit the library. The toy searches five pages for the question’s words and reads an opening time from the top two.</p></noscript>
  <RetrievalToy client:load />
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>Knowledge gets<br />a door you can open.</h2>
  <p>RAG combined learned retrieval with answer generation. External passages made some information inspectable and replaceable. The paper tested answering questions, generating questions, and checking facts.</p>
  <p>What can a model learn from a few examples placed in its prompt? Next, GPT-3 picks up a new task without any retraining.</p>
  <aside class="chapter-companions"><h3>Also in this chapter</h3><p><strong>ORQA (2019)</strong> learned retrieval and answer extraction from question-answer pairs. <strong>REALM (2020)</strong> retrieved text during pretraining to predict hidden words. <strong>DPR (2020)</strong> supplied RAG's starting retriever.</p></aside>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Two memories, two ways to mix passages</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>Retrieval is a learned match</h3>
    <p>DPR uses separate BERT encoders to turn a question x and passage z into vectors q(x) and d(z). Their dot product—multiplying corresponding entries and adding them—scores the match: <code>pη(z | x) ∝ exp(d(z) · q(x))</code>. η denotes retriever parameters. Passage vectors are computed ahead of time and stored in a searchable index. This can find matches beyond shared words; the toy only counts shared words.</p>
    <h3>One passage per answer, or per token?</h3>
    <p>A token is a piece of text. Let y be an answer, y&lt;t its earlier tokens, K the retrieved passages, and θ the generator parameters. <strong>RAG-Sequence</strong> keeps one passage for the entire answer within each possibility, then sums the possibilities:</p>
    <p><code>P(y | x) ≈ Σz∈K pη(z | x) ∏t pθ(yt | x, z, y&lt;t)</code></p>
    <p><strong>RAG-Token</strong> mixes passage-conditioned predictions at each token, allowing different passages to contribute along the answer:</p>
    <p><code>P(y | x) ≈ ∏t Σz∈K pη(z | x) pθ(yt | x, z, y&lt;t)</code></p>
    <p>Both retrieve the candidate set from the input question. RAG-Token does not run a new search at every generated token. These sums are called marginalisation: combining possibilities instead of deciding one passage must be the source.</p>
    <h3>What was trained?</h3>
    <p>The system combined a DPR retriever with BART-large, a 400-million-parameter encoder–decoder Transformer. During RAG fine-tuning, or training for a task, the question encoder and generator were updated using input–answer pairs; the passage encoder and index stayed fixed. The index contained about 21 million 100-word Wikipedia passages. The reader saw each passage concatenated with the question.</p>
    <h3>A result, and an update experiment</h3>
    <p>Table 1 reports 44.5% exact match for RAG-Sequence on the Natural Questions test set, compared with 41.5% for the DPR question-answering system. Exact match checks whether the generated answer matches an accepted reference after normalisation. These are scores for that task, not general factual accuracy.</p>
    <p>The authors also swapped Wikipedia indices from 2016 and 2018 and asked about 82 world leaders who had changed. The matching-year indices gave 70% and 68% correct answers respectively. This small test shows that replacing external knowledge can change answers without retraining; it does not establish reliable updating of every fact.</p>
    <h3>What retrieval cannot promise</h3>
    <p>Search can miss useful evidence. A retrieved passage can be outdated or misleading. The generator can still produce unsupported text, and attaching a passage does not prove an answer follows from it. The paper’s task results and limited update experiment do not establish universal factuality or deletion of knowledge from model parameters.</p>
    <h3>Inside the toy</h3>
    <p>The question’s distinct English words (when, does, moss, museum, open) are matched against each page, ignoring case, punctuation and repeats. A page earns one point per shared word; ties go to the earlier letter. The top two pages go to a fixed sentence reader, which recognises “Place opens at time.” in forms such as 9:00, 10.30 or 9am and ignores other sentences.</p>
    <p>One distinct time produces an answer, no times produce a missing-evidence message, and different times produce a conflict. So a planted page fools the reader only if it out-matches the official notice and pushes it out of the top two. Copying the question’s words into a fake page resembles a tactic studied in poisoning attacks on real retrieval systems. This deliberately cautious reader is an illustration, not RAG’s generator, and RAG searches with learned vectors rather than shared words.</p>
  </div>
</details>
